import Mathlib
import TauCeti.Algebra.AlgebraicGroup.Tangent.Cotangent

/-!
# Hodge–Tate lattices and Lubin–Tate logarithms: target signatures

This file is not the roadmap. The definitive document for this folder is `README.md`. This file
states the roadmap's headline theorems against the pinned Mathlib and Tau Ceti. Two of the four
are statable at the pins and are stated below: the cokernel bound at every prime in its finite
flat form (0.5b (a)) and the sharp radius for inverses of twisted systems of logarithms (3.1).

The two other headlines are statements about a semi-abelian scheme `G → X` over a noetherian
normal integral scheme `X` flat over `ℤ_(p)`: its sheaf of invariant differentials `ω_G`, the
quasi-finite flat group scheme `G[p^R]` and the regular functions on it that restrict to
characters of the generic `p^R`-torsion (HodgeTateAndCanonicalSubgroups §§0.2, 0.13). Neither
Mathlib nor Tau Ceti has abelian or semi-abelian schemes, their invariant differentials or
`p`-divisible groups at the pins. Tau Ceti has finite flat commutative group schemes over any
base (`TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.FiniteFlatCommGroupScheme`), the Cartier duality of finite Hopf
algebras and the cotangent module of a Hopf algebra, which give the finite-level form of the
cokernel bound stated below, but no semi-abelian schemes and no quasi-finite torsion `G[p^R]`.

* The Hodge–Tate lattice and its sandwich (0.5a, 0.5b (b)): for `R ≥ 1` the subsheaf
  `ω^{HT}_R ⊆ ω_G` generated by `p^R·ω_G` and the differentials of the regular functions on
  neighbourhoods of the unit section of `G[p^R]` that agree with characters of `A[p^R](Ω)` is
  coherent, and `κ₀·ω_G ⊆ ω^{HT}_R ⊆ ω_G` at every codimension-one point over `p` and every point
  over `p` at which it is locally free, with `κ₀ = ζ_p − 1` for odd `p` and `κ₀ = 2` for `p = 2`.
  It lacks semi-abelian schemes over a scheme, their invariant differentials and Lie algebras as
  sheaves, the torsion group schemes `G[p^R]` with their regular functions, and coherent
  subsheaves of a locally free sheaf on a scheme.
* The Hodge–Tate sequence in the special fibre (0.17a): at any prime `p`, for `R ≥ 2`, a
  principal polarisation and `ω^{HT}_R` locally free near the special fibre, the Hodge–Tate map
  of the level induces a surjection `T ⊗ O_X/p^{R−1} → ω^{HT}_R/p^{R−1}` of locally free
  `O_X/p^{R−1}`-modules whose kernel is locally a direct summand of rank `g`, equal to its own
  orthogonal for the Weil pairing and isomorphic to `Lie^{HT}_R ⊗ μ_{p^R}(Ω) ⊗ O_X/p^{R−1}`; on
  the reduced special fibre `S` this is a surjection of vector bundles with Lagrangian kernel.
  With an action of `O_E` and `p` unramified in `E` it splits into split exact sequences of
  eigen-quotients modulo `p^{R−1}`, and on the bundle of pairs of full flags over `S`, with
  `x_0, …, x_{N−1}` the first Chern classes of the graded pieces numbered from the bottom of the
  flag, the power sums of the `x_i` vanish in the integral Chow ring and, for every
  `GL_N`-dominant weight `λ`, the line bundle with first Chern class `−Σ_i λ_i x_i` is nef. It
  lacks, besides the carriers of the previous item, the Weil pairing of a principal
  polarisation of an abelian scheme, vector bundles and their flag bundles over a scheme, Chow
  rings with Chern classes, and nefness of line bundles.

The finite-level forms of 0.5d–0.5h, the kernel statement 2.4 and the `p`-divisible group of
0.18a need `p`-divisible groups over `O_C`, abelian schemes and semi-abelian schemes with their
Tate modules, and are not stated. Among the logarithms, 3.2–3.4 and 3.6 are statable over a
complete discretely valued field with a Frobenius lift; they are steps toward the cocycle
statements of the family, not headlines, and are not stated. The letter group 3.5 is a
statement about formal group laws in several variables, of which Mathlib and Tau Ceti have only
the one-dimensional `FormalGroup`.
-/

namespace TauCetiRoadmap.RiemannHypothesisForDirichletLFunctions.HodgeTateLatticesAndLubinTateLogarithms

/-! ## Layer 0: Hodge–Tate lattices and cokernel bounds at finite level -/

/-- The cokernel bound at every prime, in its finite flat form (0.5b (a)). Let `C` be a complete
algebraically closed nonarchimedean field of characteristic `0` with valuation ring `O_C`, `p` a
prime with `|p| < 1`, and `A` the Hopf algebra of a finite flat commutative group scheme of
`p`-power order over `O_C`: a commutative and cocommutative Hopf `O_C`-algebra, finite projective
of rank `p^n`. Every `κ ∈ O_C` with `|κ|^(p−1) ≤ |p|` multiplies the cotangent module
`ω = I/I²` of `A` into the span of the classes of `u − 1` for the group-like elements `u` of `A`,
which are the values of the Hodge–Tate map of the Cartier dual. For odd `p` this is Fargues'
bound; at `p = 2` the factor `κ = 2` qualifies. -/
theorem smul_mem_span_cotangentMap_groupLike (C : Type*) [NormedField C] [IsUltrametricDist C]
    [CompleteSpace C] [IsAlgClosed C] [CharZero C]
    (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (hnormp : ‖(p : C)‖ < 1)
    (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [HopfAlgebra (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A]
    [Coalgebra.IsCocomm (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A]
    [Module.Finite (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A]
    [Module.Projective (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A] (n : ℕ)
    (horder : Module.finrank (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A = p ^ n)
    (κ : (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring)
    (hκ : ‖(κ : C)‖ ^ (p - 1) ≤ ‖(p : C)‖)
    (x : TauCeti.Bialgebra.CotangentSpace (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A) :
    κ • x ∈ Submodule.span (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring
      (Set.range fun u : GroupLike (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A =>
        TauCeti.Bialgebra.cotangentMap (NormedField.valuation (K := C)).valuationSubring A
          (u : A)) := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer 3: Logarithms of Lubin–Tate laws in several variables -/

open scoped Classical in
/-- The twisted system of logarithms of 3.1. For a set `S` with a self-map `ς`, an integer
`q ≥ 2` and `x ∈ S`, the series `λ_x(T) = Σ_{e ≥ 0} T_{ς^e(x)}^{q^e} / p^e` in the variables
`T_y`, `y ∈ S`: its coefficient at the monomial `T_{ς^e(x)}^{q^e}` is `p^{−e}`, and every other
coefficient is `0`. A monomial `T_y^{q^e}` has degree `q^e > e`, so the sum below has at most one
nonzero term when `q ≥ 2`. -/
noncomputable def twistedLog (K : Type*) [Field K] (p : ℕ) {S : Type*} (ς : S → S) (q : ℕ)
    (x : S) : MvPowerSeries S K :=
  fun n => ∑ e ∈ Finset.range (Finsupp.degree n + 1),
    if n = Finsupp.single (ς^[e] x) (q ^ e) then ((p : K) ^ e)⁻¹ else 0

/-- Sharp radius for inverses of twisted systems of logarithms (3.1). Let `K` be a field of
characteristic `0` with a nonarchimedean absolute value normalised by `|p| = p⁻¹`, `S` a finite
set with a self-map `ς` and `q ≥ 2`. There is a unique family `g = (g_x)_{x ∈ S}` of series
without constant term with `λ_x(g) = T_x` for all `x`; it also satisfies `g_x(λ) = T_x`; and
`v_p(coeff_n g_x) ≥ −(|n| − 1)/(q − 1)`, that is `‖coeff_n g_x‖ ≤ p^((|n| − 1)/(q − 1))`, for
every multi-index `n ≠ 0` of total degree `|n|`.
[FormalProof] has the case `q = p²`; the general `q` is this roadmap's, by the same route. -/
theorem exists_twistedLog_inverse_norm_coeff_le (K : Type*) [NormedField K] [IsUltrametricDist K]
    [CharZero K] (p : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (hpK : ‖(p : K)‖ = (p : ℝ)⁻¹)
    {S : Type*} [Finite S] (ς : S → S) (q : ℕ) (hq : 2 ≤ q) :
    ∃ g : S → MvPowerSeries S K,
      (∀ x, MvPowerSeries.constantCoeff (g x) = 0) ∧
      (∀ x, MvPowerSeries.subst g (twistedLog K p ς q x) = MvPowerSeries.X x) ∧
      (∀ g' : S → MvPowerSeries S K, (∀ x, MvPowerSeries.constantCoeff (g' x) = 0) →
        (∀ x, MvPowerSeries.subst g' (twistedLog K p ς q x) = MvPowerSeries.X x) → g' = g) ∧
      (∀ x, MvPowerSeries.subst (twistedLog K p ς q) (g x) = MvPowerSeries.X x) ∧
      ∀ x (n : S →₀ ℕ), n ≠ 0 →
        ‖MvPowerSeries.coeff n (g x)‖ ≤
          (p : ℝ) ^ ((((Finsupp.degree n : ℕ) : ℝ) - 1) / ((q : ℝ) - 1)) := by
  sorry

end TauCetiRoadmap.RiemannHypothesisForDirichletLFunctions.HodgeTateLatticesAndLubinTateLogarithms
